# Resolution
Fixed by `abb8982 Validate multipart completion part placement`.

What this commit fixed:
- Made completed multipart object-part placement an explicit validation authority by adding `expected_object_parts` to `BuildCompleteMultipartObjectCommandReq`.
- Derived the expected completed `ObjectPartRecord` values from topology at the cluster call site before invoking the object-mutation metadata client.
- Derived the same expected object-part placement in the storage-node server before delegating the RPC request to the local storage-node client.
- Tightened `UnixStorageNodeClient::validate_complete_multipart_command_response` to require returned `commit.parts` to exactly match the expected object parts, including `data_pg_id`.
- Kept the local builder honest by rejecting requests whose supplied expected object parts do not match the storage-node topology-derived parts.
- Added a regression that mutates only `ObjectPartRecord.data_pg_id` in an otherwise valid complete-multipart response and verifies the Unix client fails closed.

What was already fixed or not changed by this commit:
- The existing validator already checked route identity, command payload kind, bucket/key/upload/generation identity, version id, object metadata, selected/omitted cleanup completeness, terminal stream cleanup, bucket write reservation proof, and stale null-version payload identity. Those checks remain in place.
- `CommitMultipartObjectCommand::matches_request` still compares the uploaded multipart part identity fields available from `MultipartPartRecord`; it intentionally does not own topology placement validation because `data_pg_id` is not present in the selected upload part row.
- PgStore still persists `ObjectPartRecord.data_pg_id` into `object_parts`; the fix is to reject malformed remote command-build responses before publishing/applying them.

