# Summary
A security-relevant bug was introduced: the commit adds a remote multipart completion command builder and response validation, but the validation is incomplete because it omits the topology-derived data_pg_id field for completed multipart object parts.
The new Unix/RPC path for building CompleteMultipartUpload metadata commands validates most fields of the remote MetadataCommandEnvelope before publishing it. However, the validation relies on CommitMultipartObjectCommand::matches_request plus a bucket/key/version_id identity check for each returned ObjectPartRecord. Neither check verifies ObjectPartRecord.data_pg_id, even though the local builder derives data_pg_id from the cluster topology and PgStore persists that value into the object manifest. A faulty or malicious remote object-metadata primary can therefore return a command whose part metadata matches the requested uploaded parts but points the completed object's manifest at the wrong data placement group. Once the coordinator accepts and applies that command, later reads may fetch missing or incorrect shards, causing object corruption or unavailability. This does not bypass S3 authentication directly, but it weakens the intended validation at the new remote storage-node trust boundary.

