## Report
Validated the finding against commit 25a204e4. I built the storage crate test binary with debug symbols and injected a targeted PoC into the existing complete-multipart response validation unit test. The PoC clones an otherwise-valid CommitMultipartObject response, changes only commit.parts[0].data_pg_id to 424242, then calls UnixStorageNodeClient::validate_complete_multipart_command_response. The command was accepted: cargo test output included `AutoValidator PoC: validation result for mismatched data_pg_id=424242: Ok(())` and the test passed. Crash attempt: no crash occurred or was expected because this is a trust-boundary logic-validation bug, not memory corruption. Valgrind attempt: valgrind was not installed in the container. Debugger validation: LLDB stopped inside validate_complete_multipart_command_response at crates/storage/src/node_client.rs:6360, the loop that checks only part.bucket, part.key, and part.version_id. The first valid call showed `part->data_pg_id = 0`; the second PoC call showed `part->data_pg_id = 424242`, with the backtrace returning to the PoC validation call at node_client.rs:12465. Code evidence: the remote complete-multipart RPC path uses object_metadata_command_build_request and validate_complete_multipart_command_response before accepting the command at crates/storage/src/node_client.rs:5020-5048. The validator delegates to CommitMultipartObjectCommand::matches_request at node_client.rs:6333-6353, then only checks bucket/key/version_id for each ObjectPartRecord at node_client.rs:6359-6369. matches_request compares part_number, size, etag, etag_kind, part_okh, part_vid, ec_k, ec_m, and checksum, but omits ObjectPartRecord.data_pg_id at crates/storage/src/metadata_command.rs:630-658. The trusted local builder derives data_pg_id from topology at crates/storage/src/node_client.rs:9180-9207. Accepted CommitMultipartObject commands are applied through PgStore::apply_commit_multipart_object_command, which passes command.parts into put_multipart_object_explicit_in_open_txn at crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:7236-7281; that function inserts part.data_pg_id into object_parts at pg_store.rs:10347-10381, and object part reads select data_pg_id back from object_parts at pg_store.rs:15543-15553. This confirms that a malicious/faulty remote metadata primary can supply a completed multipart manifest with an arbitrary data_pg_id that survives validation and is persisted.

